    {
      "id": "Sr8oedIPi",
      "draft": true,
      "fields": {
        "Title": "Putkimiina M 68",
        "Slug": "fragment-putkimiina-m-68",
        "Content": [
          {
            "type": "TextBlock",
            "id": "v:Sr8oedIPi/cNjz4Zsot:0",
            "attributes": {
              "tag": "p"
            },
            "children": [
              {
                "type": "TextRun",
                "id": "v:Sr8oedIPi/cNjz4Zsot:0:0",
                "attributes": {
                  "text": "The Fragments sculptures explore of our ideas of beauty representing not only of the fusion between the classical and the contemporary but also remnants of our civilisations; broken by the destructive nature of our history, but never the less intact."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "TextBlock",
            "id": "v:Sr8oedIPi/cNjz4Zsot:1",
            "attributes": {
              "tag": "p"
            },
            "children": [
              {
                "type": "TextRun",
                "id": "v:Sr8oedIPi/cNjz4Zsot:1:0",
                "attributes": {
                  "text": "The Fragments seek to draw attention to the idea of aesthetics by creating purposefully de-constructed sculptures. Working in clay, the Fragments are deliberately damaged presenting a haunting perception of history. In a number of different ways this artwork represents notions of “reverse creativity” intended to reach out to an audience with a voice but more importantly with a purpose. They comment on the glorification of the act of destruction while honouring the enduring nature to survive."
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "Gallery": "[\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/WWQgMRQWVBCavYY9EPpPVTxWbTU.jpg\",\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/UVGOonxJYhIalSru1t0w9GsDB8w.jpg\",\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/wV0wNdUgBxGfgCUlJuFkN3wIOw.jpg\",\"https://framerusercontent.com/images/kmOKaLSwPlVnn2b3sxPYh2xwmhI.jpg\"]",
